---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import UiLabel from "../../components/UiLabel.astro";
import HelpCard from "../../components/HelpCard.astro";

// Issue #1106, guide 7: using the browser version — what the browser
// keeps, signing out, and shared computers, based on the app's own web
// disclosure copy (#831's resolution). The disclosure strings are quoted
// from the ARB through <UiLabel>, the sync explanation reuses the in-app
// help card, and every factual claim carries a `<!-- cite: ... -->`
// ledger entry checked by `site/scripts/check-claims.mjs`.
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Using the browser version"
  description="What the browser version of lunarlog keeps and clears, how signing out works, and how to use it safely on a shared computer."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Using the browser version</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      lunarlog runs in a browser at
      <a href="https://app.lunarlog.app" rel="noopener">app.lunarlog.app</a>
      — the same app, with one honest difference you should understand
      before signing in on one: where a phone keeps your data under the
      operating system's encryption, a browser keeps its copy unencrypted.
      This page is exactly that difference, in the app's own words.
      <!-- cite: lib/ui/web/dev_banner.dart (the two honest browser states); PRIVACY.md#6-security-protections -->
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>What the browser keeps</h2>
      <p>
        Signing in to lunarlog in a browser shows a one-time notice first,
        and it does not mince words. In the app's own disclosure:
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        <UiLabel key="webFirstRunSyncedBody" />
      </p>
      <p>
        A small banner saying the same — <UiLabel
          key="webBannerSyncedCopy"
        /> — stays available while you use the browser version. The rule of
        thumb it teaches: treat a browser session as the copy, not the
        original. Your account's data lives in your account; the browser
        holds a working copy of it while you are signed in.
        <!-- cite: lib/ui/web/dev_banner.dart (synced-state banner + first-run acknowledgement); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (webBannerSyncedCopy, webFirstRunSyncedBody) -->
      </p>
      <HelpCard id="sync-what" />
      <p>
        The phone apps differ in one more way that matters here: a phone
        keeps everything behind its screen lock, while the browser version
        does not add a lock of its own. Anyone at the keyboard with the
        session open can read what the browser holds — which is why the
        next section is short and strict.
        <!-- cite: lib/app_root.dart (gate.requiresUnlock is false on web); lib/startup/gate/local_auth_gate.dart (native always locks) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Shared computers</h2>
      <p>
        On a computer other people use, the habit is simple: sign out
        when you are done, every time.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Signing out removes the copy from that browser — your account's
          data stays in your account and can sync again later.
          <!-- cite: lib/l10n/app_en.arb (webFirstRunSyncedBody, webBannerSyncedCopy); lib/ui/web/dev_banner.dart -->
        </li>
        <li>
          For a stronger clear, the browser version has
          <UiLabel key="webWipeAction" />, behind a confirmation that names
          the consequence: <UiLabel key="webWipeConfirmSyncedBody" />
          Confirm with <UiLabel key="webWipeConfirmAction" />.
          <!-- cite: lib/ui/web/dev_banner.dart (confirm-guarded wipe, KTD16 device reset); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (webWipe*) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          Signing out of the account on all devices at once — from
          <UiLabel key="settingsTitle" /> under
          <UiLabel key="accountSectionTitle" /> — also clears this
          device's copy, though other devices may keep working for up to
          an hour: <UiLabel key="accountSignOutEverywhereBody" />
          <!-- cite: lib/ui/settings/settings_screen.dart (account section); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (accountSignOutEverywhereBody) -->
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        And the judgment call no guide can make for you: a cycle log for a
        teenager belongs on her own devices. A shared family computer in a
        common room, used with her knowledge, is one thing; a borrowed
        work laptop is another. When in doubt, use your phone.
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md#5-minors-privacy--family-profiles (minors' profiles stay with their guardians) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What the browser version is good for</h2>
      <p>
        A personal computer with a password-protected account of your own:
        comfortable keyboard-and-screen editing, catching up on a household's
        profiles from a laptop, or trying lunarlog before installing
        anything. All the everyday things — logging, reading history,
        reading estimates — work the same as on a phone; when you go back
        to your phone, the same account puts the same profiles there.
        <!-- cite: docs/product/positioning.md (an account is the product's core loop); PRIVACY.md#1-core-principles (resilient offline posture applies to the native apps) -->
      </p>
      <p>
        If the build you open has accounts switched off entirely — a
        development build — the app says so in place, and keeps its banner
        up: not for real data. That banner, too, is the app being honest.
        <!-- cite: lib/ui/web/dev_banner.dart (sync-off state, webBannerDevCopy); lib/config.dart (LUNARLOG_WEB_SYNC) -->
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
